                "quoted_status_profile_id": null,
                "quoted_status_profile_username": null,
                "reply_count": 19,
                "retweet_count": 7,
                "source": "Twitter Web Client",
                "text": "The budget reconciliation package updates the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) by returning to mandatory work requirements and tighter accountability designed to strengthen the program and ensure it serves those who need it most.\n\nWork requirements now cover more adults, including parents with children 14 or older. These changes push for greater participation in work, volunteering, or education programs. Waivers from these rules will be limited to areas with high unemployment, making sure states like California can't exempt the entire state from the work requirement.\n\nBenefit increases going forward will be capped to track inflation, preventing unchecked growth without Congressional approval.\n\nFor the first time, states will be responsible for a portion of actual SNAP food benefit costs, not just administrative expenses, encouraging stronger financial stewardship at the state level. The formula for state matching funds will be updated to reflect recent payment error rates, promoting better oversight and reducing waste. When states don't have skin in the game to ensure payment accuracy, the high error rates hurt SNAP recipients the most.\n\nThese changes are about making SNAP more accountable, sustainable, and focused on helping Americans get back to work.\n\nMore details about changes to SNAP can be found at the link below: https://t.co/W3a5k7W3eN",
                "text_lang": "en",
                "text_tagged_users": null,
                "text_tagged_users_ids": null,
